# SYNOPSIS

```text
echo start loinnir-test >/svc/launcher/ctl
```

# DESCRIPTION

loinnir-test is a native program that draws through Loinnir (loinnir(2)) on whichever core backend the GPU class offers (gpucore(2)), reads the results back through host-visible memory and judges them.
It needs `/dev` from devmgr, as every GPU program does.

It first draws a triangle through the root pointer, once with a constant colour (a fragment shader of no inputs) and once with a colour from the vertices, into a 64 by 64 image cleared to blue.
Every pixel more than a pixel from the diagonal must be exactly the triangle's colour if inside and exactly the clear if outside.
Then it samples a texture from the heap by index over a 32 by 32 target, and checks that two submissions count up the timeline.
Its lines say `loinnir-test: ok: Loinnir drew a triangle on the core` once the first draws.

On the Vulkan backend (Venus under QEMU, or a host's Vulkan) it goes on to the core's full feature set.
It first prints the device's name, its limits, and the feature flags in groups (queues and draws, images, samplers, pipeline state, commands, shaders, viewports and sparse), each as the flags the device has, a slash, and the ones it lacks: `loinnir-test: features images: ... / ...`.
Then it runs these cases, each of which prints `loinnir-test: ok: core: ...` and what it judged, or `loinnir-test: skip: core: ...` and why:

- depth: four rectangles at different depths, drawn out of order with the depth test, and one whose depth crosses another's; each pixel against the nearest rectangle there (a pixel where two depths are within 0.005 is not judged);
- stencil: a mask built by replacing and incrementing the stencil value in two overlapping rectangles, then two draws that pass where it equals 2 and 1, the second with the reference set by the pass where the device has dynamic state; exact;
- depth kept and stencil kept: the depth and stencil cases again, the first draws in a pass that stores the plane and the rest in a second that keeps the colour and the plane (`depthLoad`, `stencilLoad`), against the same reference; exact;
- msaa: a rectangle whose edge is half way through a pixel, drawn with 4 samples and resolved once by the pass and once by `resolveImage`; the covered pixels and the clear are exact, and the edge pixel is the mean of both within 2;
  then the same at every sample count the device offers (the edge pixel the mean of the samples left of the edge on Vulkan's standard locations, a sample on the edge either way), a target stored and resolved by one pass and its samples resolved again by `resolveImage`, a target kept by a second pass that draws beside it, the sample mask (samples 0 and 2 of 4: half and half), sample shading with a shader whose colour is its sample's own (`gl_SampleID`), alpha to one (alpha 0.25 stored as 1), a depth attachment of 4 samples resolved (sample 0) by the pass, discarded and stored, read back exactly, and alpha to coverage (alpha 0 covers nothing and 1 everything, exactly; what 0.5 covers is printed, `loinnir-test: alpha to coverage:`, and must be some samples but not all), each within 2 of the mean;
- layers: a draw of an instance a layer (Layer from the vertex stage, red 20 times the layer) into 3 layers and into 10 (two of the Mali's tiler contexts), a second pass that keeps the 3 and draws into the first, a clear of a rectangle of layers 1 and 2 of 3 inside a pass, and a pass into a view of layer 1, level 1 of an image of 2 layers and 2 levels; every texel of every layer and level exact;
- multiview: a pass of views 1 and 3 (mask 0b1010) into 4 layers cleared black, a vertex shader that moves each view 2 pixels and gives it red 20 times the view; each view's layer its own rectangle and colour, the others black; then a second pass keeps the views and clears a corner in both; exact;
- cull: two rectangles wound opposite ways in four tiles, drawn with back, front and no culling and with the other front face; exact;
- blend: alpha over, additive, multiply, subtract, reverse subtract, min, max, source colour factors, constant colour (static and dynamic), alpha saturate, a write mask and, where there are logic operations, xor, each in its own tile against the formula in floating point, within 1 (the hardware rounds its own way); xor is exact;
- mips: a texture of four levels each a different colour, sampled at levels 0 to 3 by a sampler that takes the nearest level (exact), and at 1.5 by one that blends levels (the mean of levels 1 and 2, within 2);
- volume: a 4 by 4 by 4 texture, one colour a slice, read at each slice's depth; exact;
- cube: a cube texture, one colour a face, read along each axis; exact;
- 1d: a 1D texture of eight colours, read by a quad eight pixels wide, each column its own texel; exact;
- swizzle: a BGRA image read through an RGBA view that swaps red and blue, magnified by a nearest sampler; exact;
- custom border: a red texture sampled over three times its width by a sampler whose border is a colour of its own, which the outer thirds are (within 1), and the middle the texture's, exact; skipped where the device has no custom border colours (under Venus, where the host's device lacks VK_EXT_custom_border_color or the renderer doesn't carry it);
- texel buffers: a compute shader that fetches 16 texels from a uniform texel buffer, stores three times each, plus its index, to a storage texel buffer and gives each an atomic add of one, through the heap's texel-buffer arrays; read back from the buffer's own memory, exact;
- viewports: one rectangle over the whole of clip space drawn twice into 16 by 16, instance i with viewport i (ViewportIndex from the vertex stage), the first viewport the left half cut by a scissor to the top-left quarter (red there), the second the right half cut to rows 4 on (green there); every pixel exact; skipped without `multiViewport`, two viewports and `shaderOutputViewportIndex`;
- sparse buffer: a sparse buffer of three of the device's pages; with residency, pages 0 and 2 bound to the second and first page of memory and page 1 left unbound, each bound page filled with its own word, then page 0 unbound and page 1 bound to the memory page 0 had: page 1 reads page 0's word, page 2 its own, and page 0 zero where the device reads unbound pages as zero; without residency, all three bound and filled, then pages 0 and 2 swap their memory pages; every 61st word judged, exact; the memory is reached only through the sparse buffer; skipped without `sparseBinding`;
- sparse image: an rgba8 image of four of the device's blocks used partly bound, its top-left and bottom-right blocks bound to two pages and copied red and green, sampled with the nearest filter into 8 by 8: each quarter its block's colour, exact (the unbound quarters zero where the device reads unbound blocks as zero); skipped without `sparseResidencyImage2D`, or where level 0 is in the mip tail;
- copy region, blit and clears: an image region copied to another offset, a blit at twice the size and flipped, a clear of an image, of a rectangle inside a pass, a buffer filled and updated, and a buffer copied in and out of an image by regions with a row length; each exact.
- transfers: a pass, a copy of its region, a flipped blit of that and a second pass that keeps it, all in one submission, and an update, a fill and a copy of memory at odd offsets, each reading what the one before wrote; buffer regions of 1, 4 and 8-byte texels with a row length of 13 and an image height of 5 into level 1 of two layers; `copyImage` from a level of two layers onto another image at offsets, and a block of a volume between slices; a depth and stencil image (`depth24stencil8` or `depth32fstencil8`) cleared by ranges and aspects, both aspects copied in at an offset, onto another image and out, and blitted at twice the size flipped; a compressed format's blocks copied at block offsets and into a second level; blits that filter (linear into `rgba16float`, flipped, within 0.01), widen integers (`rgba8uint` into `rgba16uint`, flipped, exact) and encode sRGB (within 1); levels 1 and 2 of layers 1 and 2 cleared and the rest kept; a region of a 4-sample image resolved at offsets (the edge within 2). Each is skipped where the device lacks its features or formats.

On the Mali backend a last resource case runs 97 passes of 10 layers (two tiler contexts) in a row, the last judged exactly, and requires the tiler heap's context and its chunk's header to read the same after 48 of them as after one pass of one layer; it then prints those words after 48 more passes made to hand back only the last context's chunks (`loinnir-test: heap words`), a probe of whether that leak shows.
The lines of the multisampling, layers, clear, copy, blit, transfer and kept cases, and every skip, are printed again after the resource cases, as the board's serial console drops lines.

A case runs only if the device reports the features it uses, and is skipped, with the missing feature named, if it doesn't.

On the Mali backend it runs the checks of the core's features the device has, in CoreFeatures.swift: indexed and indirect draws, kept attachments, indirect dispatch, occlusion predicates, storage images, atomics, timestamps and the texel buffers, each judged exactly.
Where the device claims `shaderAtomic32`, 16384 invocations add 1 to a counter, add their index to a sum and take the maximum of it, in memory the CPU wrote a nonzero start into and then again after the CPU wrote over the result, and one dispatch stores beside 256 adds; with `fillBuffer`, the same in device memory the GPU fills and copies out; each word exact.
Where it claims `shaderImageAtomics` and `r32uint` storage with atomics, 64 x 64 invocations add to 64 texels of an r32uint storage image and take the maximum of their index into 64 more, over a start the CPU copied in, and every texel is judged.
It also runs the resource cases of ResourceCases.swift, among them import and present: memory and a window's image exported and imported again in the one process, and a window's frames made by `makePresentImage` 64, 80, 112, 40 and 104 pixels wide (rows of 256, 320 and 448 bytes, and 160 and 416 padded to 192 and 448), drawn by the GPU, read through the window's handle mapped as Radharc maps it with the frame's stride, and imported with that stride as another image and sampled, each pixel exact (an import of the unpadded rows must be refused), and the timeline's counter (`timelineHandle`), which must read at least the value last signalled and refuse an add (`loinnir-test: ok: present: ...`).
The texel-buffer checks are two compute shaders through the heap's arrays: one fetches four views (r32float, rgba8, rg32float and r8) as a vec4 and the sizes of each, and every channel is judged, among them the channels a format lacks, which must read (R, 0, 0, 1) and (R, G, 0, 1); the other reads a storage texel buffer, stores to it and reads again, in program order.
Where the device claims atomics on texel buffers a third check fetches, stores and adds atomically; a device that does not claim them prints what an atomic add did as a probe (`loinnir-test: atomic probe:`) and is not judged on it.
A case whose commands the device refuses with `.unsupported` is skipped with the device's words, and the log shows it; any other failure of a call, and any pixel outside its tolerance, fails the program.
Each case prints how long it took, and a last line says how many passed and were skipped, with the total time.

On the board (the Mali backend) it does the first three only, and says that the others are judged by that backend's own tests.
It prints `loinnir-test: passed` and exits 0 if nothing failed, and `loinnir-test: FAILED:` and what failed, and exits 1, otherwise.

In boot-venus's image it is a service that runs beside vulkan-test(8) and compose-test, which waits for it and fails if it did not exit with code 0; board-test(8) starts it.

